WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.750
After all that, a warning from the Coast Guard itself.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.250
Don’t trust buoys blindly.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.350
A buoy is a floating object on a chain.

4
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.075
It can drag in a storm, get hit by a barge, capsize, or sink.

5
00:00:14.325 --> 00:00:16.725
Lights fail, and colors fade.

6
00:00:16.975 --> 00:00:21.000
So use buoys as a guide, never as a fixed position.

7
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.225
What do you check them against?

8
00:00:24.225 --> 00:00:27.450
The chart, the depth sounder, and your GPS.

9
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:31.725
If the buoy and the chart disagree, believe the water depth.

10
00:00:31.975 --> 00:00:36.875
If you find an aid off station or not working, report it to the Coast Guard.

11
00:00:37.125 --> 00:00:43.250
And know about private aids, called PATONs, put out by marinas and property owners.

12
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.125
They follow the same colors and shapes, but they may not be maintained to the same standard.

13
00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:51.300
That’s lesson three.

14
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.650
Red, right, returning.

15
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.000
Nuns even, cans odd.

16
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.075
Follow the rear mark.

17
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:00.800
And trust the water, not the buoy.

18
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.275
Next time: the marks that aren’t red or green.
